    <!-- Main Content Overlapping Hero -->
    <main class="flex-1 max-w-4xl mx-auto w-full px-6 py-8 -mt-16 relative z-20 macos-glass mb-12">
        
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-4"><span class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-white/10 text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-300 text-xs font-bold px-3 py-1 rounded-full uppercase tracking-wider flex items-center gap-1"><svg class="w-3 h-3" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 8v4l3 3m6-3a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg> 2 MIN READ</span></div><div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-8 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10 pb-6">
            <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">Maturity</h2>
            <div class="w-24 h-1 rounded-full opacity-80" style="background-color: #f97316"></div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="prose  max-w-none">
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>What Is Spiritual Maturity?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Let's be honest about what maturity is not: it's not how many Bible verses you can quote from memory, how many years you've been saved, how many conferences you've attended, or how polished your Instagram testimony looks. Spiritual maturity is Christlikeness — plain and simple. It's becoming more like Jesus in how you love, how you respond under pressure, and how you treat the people who can't do anything for you.</p><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Galatians 5:22-23 lays out the evidence clearly: the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, and self-control. Notice — it's fruit, not performance metrics. You don't manufacture fruit; it grows naturally when you're connected to the right source.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Maturity is measured by what comes out of you when life squeezes you, not by what you put on display when things are comfortable. A mature believer can be wronged and still choose forgiveness. A mature believer can walk through uncertainty and still trust God.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">That's the goal we're chasing together.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Dying to Self</h3>
                <div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">The hardest part of the Christian life is not the doing — it's the dying. Jesus didn't sugarcoat it. Luke 9:23 records His words with piercing clarity: "If anyone would come after me, let him deny himself, take up his cross daily, and follow me." Daily.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Not once at an altar call. Not once a year at a retreat. Every single day, you wake up and choose to die to your preferences, your ego, your need to be right, your desire for control, and your craving for validation.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">This is the part of faith nobody posts about — the death of self that makes room for the life of Christ. Maturity means your emotions don't lead you; the Spirit does. It means you can feel angry and not sin.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">You can feel lonely and not compromise. You can feel overlooked and not grow bitter. Dying to self doesn't mean you stop having feelings — it means your feelings stop having you.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">The cross wasn't just something Jesus carried for us; it's something He invites us to carry with Him. And on the other side of that death is a resurrection life that the world cannot explain or take away.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Handling Suffering</h3>
                <div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">Nobody signs up for suffering, but James 1:2-4 says something that sounds almost offensive until the Holy Spirit opens your eyes: "Consider it pure joy, my brothers and sisters, whenever you face trials of many kinds, because you know that the testing of your faith produces perseverance. Let perseverance finish its work so that you may be mature and complete, not lacking anything." The painful seasons of your life — the breakup, the loss, the betrayal, the diagnosis, the rejection — are not punishments from an angry God. They are training grounds supervised by a loving Father who is forming you into something unshakable.</p></div><div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">God uses pressure the way a sculptor uses a chisel: not to destroy you, but to reveal the masterpiece He's been working on all along. Romans 8:28 promises that God works all things together for the good of those who love Him and are called according to His purpose. Not some things — all things.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">The suffering you're walking through right now has a purpose, even when you can't see it. Don't waste your pain. Let it do its work.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">On the other side of this valley, you'll have a testimony that sets someone else free.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="mb-8">
                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mb-4 text-gray-900 dark:text-white flex items-center gap-3"><svg class="w-6 h-6 opacity-70" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m6 2a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>Reproducing Yourself</h3>
                <div class="p-6 bg-gray-50 hover:bg-gray-100 dark:hover:bg-white/10 transition-all duration-300 transform hover:-translate-y-1 hover:shadow-lg dark:bg-white/5 rounded-2xl border-l-4 my-6" style="border-color: currentColor"><p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 text-lg font-medium leading-relaxed italic">The ultimate sign of spiritual maturity is not how much you know — it's whether you're reproducing what you know in someone else. 2 Timothy 2:2 gives us the blueprint: "The things you have heard me say in the presence of many witnesses, entrust to reliable people who will also be qualified to teach others." That's four generations of discipleship in one verse — Paul to Timothy, Timothy to reliable people, reliable people to others. This is God's strategy for reaching the world, and it runs through you.</p></div><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">Mature believers don't just consume sermons, podcasts, and Bible studies — they contribute. They pour into others what was poured into them. They grab a younger believer, buy them coffee, open the Word together, and say, "Let me show you what God showed me." Are you making disciples? Not just attending church — but actually investing in someone else's spiritual growth? That's the question maturity demands.</p><p class="text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 text-lg leading-relaxed mb-4">You were never meant to hoard the grace you've received. You were meant to multiply it. The faith that was handed to you is meant to be handed through you to the next generation.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        
        <div class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200 dark:border-white/10">
